<section xml:id="sec-mod7-worked-examples">
  <title>Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>
    <p>
      Read each example slowly. The goal is not just to see what the
      answer is, but to internalize the proof <em>template</em>.
      Example 1 shows how to compute a derivative directly from the
      limit definition. Example 2 proves differentiability implies
      continuity&#x2014;a result that looks obvious but requires a
      specific algebraic trick. Example 3 is a full proof of Rolle's
      Theorem, the key lemma for the MVT. Example 4 demonstrates the
      MVT used to produce an inequality, the most common application
      pattern in MAT 5610.
    </p>
  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1: DERIVATIVE FROM THE DEFINITION
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod7-we-1">
    <title>Derivative from the Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>f(x) = x^3</m> is differentiable at every
        <m>a \in \R</m> and that <m>f'(a) = 3a^2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Compute the difference quotient <m>(f(a+h) - f(a))/h</m>,
        simplify the numerator algebraically, cancel the factor of
        <m>h</m>, and evaluate the resulting limit as <m>h \to 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Scratch work.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Expand the numerator:
        <md>(a+h)^3 - a^3 = a^3 + 3a^2h + 3ah^2 + h^3 - a^3
        = 3a^2 h + 3ah^2 + h^3 = h(3a^2 + 3ah + h^2).</md>
        Dividing by <m>h</m> leaves <m>3a^2 + 3ah + h^2</m>, which
        tends to <m>3a^2</m> as <m>h \to 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>a \in \R</m>. For <m>h \neq 0</m>, compute:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}
          \amp= \frac{(a+h)^3 - a^3}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{3a^2 h + 3ah^2 + h^3}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 3a^2 + 3ah + h^2.</mrow>
        </md>
        By the sum and product rules for limits (Module 6),
        <md>\lim_{h \to 0} (3a^2 + 3ah + h^2) = 3a^2 + 0 + 0 = 3a^2.</md>
        By the definition of the derivative, <m>f'(a) = 3a^2</m>. Since
        <m>a \in \R</m> was arbitrary, <m>f</m> is differentiable at
        every point and <m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        The entire work is in the algebra of the numerator: expanding
        <m>(a+h)^3 - a^3</m> and identifying the factor of <m>h</m>
        that cancels the denominator. Once that cancellation is done,
        the limit is immediate from the limit laws. This is the standard
        template for every computation of a derivative from the
        definition: <em>simplify, cancel, then take the limit</em>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2: DIFFERENTIABILITY IMPLIES CONTINUITY
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod7-we-2">
    <title>Differentiability Implies Continuity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that if <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>a</m>, then
        <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Write <m>f(x) - f(a)</m> as a product involving the difference
        quotient, then apply the product rule for limits. The key
        algebraic insight is that we can insert the factor
        <m>(x - a)</m> in the denominator as long as we also put it
        in the numerator.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>a</m>. For
        <m>x \neq a</m>, we may write
        <md>f(x) - f(a) = \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} \cdot (x - a).</md>
        As <m>x \to a</m>, the first factor tends to <m>f'(a)</m> by
        the hypothesis of differentiability, and the second factor
        tends to <m>0</m>. By the product rule for limits,
        <md>\lim_{x \to a} [f(x) - f(a)]
        = f'(a) \cdot 0 = 0.</md>
        Hence <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = f(a)</m>, so <m>f</m> is
        continuous at <m>a</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        The key algebraic trick is writing <m>f(x) - f(a)</m> as a
        product with the difference quotient. This is valid for
        <m>x \neq a</m>, and the limit as <m>x \to a</m> only looks
        at <m>x \neq a</m> anyway. The theorem is one-directional:
        continuity does <em>not</em> imply differentiability. The
        function <m>f(x) = |x|</m> is continuous at <m>0</m> but not
        differentiable there (the left-hand and right-hand limits of
        the difference quotient both exist but are <m>-1</m> and
        <m>+1</m> respectively, so the limit does not exist).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3: PROOF OF ROLLE'S THEOREM
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod7-we-3">
    <title>Proof of Rolle's Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove Rolle's Theorem: if <m>f</m> is continuous on
        <m>[a, b]</m>, differentiable on <m>(a, b)</m>, and
        <m>f(a) = f(b)</m>, then there exists <m>c \in (a, b)</m>
        with <m>f'(c) = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        The Extreme Value Theorem (Module 6) guarantees that
        <m>f</m> attains its maximum and minimum on <m>[a, b]</m>.
        If <m>f</m> is constant, we are immediately done. If not,
        at least one extremum must occur at an interior point
        <m>c \in (a, b)</m>, and at an interior extremum of a
        differentiable function, the derivative must be zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 1: Handle the trivial case.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(i) If <m>f</m> is constant on <m>[a, b]</m>.</em>
        Then <m>f'(c) = 0</m> for every <m>c \in (a, b)</m>, and
        the conclusion holds immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 2: Handle the nontrivial case.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(ii) If <m>f</m> is not constant on <m>[a, b]</m>.</em>
        By the Extreme Value Theorem (EVT, Module 6), <m>f</m> attains
        both its maximum and its minimum on <m>[a, b]</m>. Since
        <m>f</m> is not constant, there is some <m>x_0 \in [a, b]</m>
        with <m>f(x_0) \neq f(a)</m>, and since <m>f(a) = f(b)</m>,
        this <m>x_0</m> is not an endpoint, so <m>x_0 \in (a, b)</m>.
        There are two possibilities.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>If <m>f(x_0) \gt f(a)</m>:</em> the maximum value of
        <m>f</m> is at least <m>f(x_0)</m>, which is greater than
        <m>f(a) = f(b)</m>, so the maximum is not attained at
        <m>a</m> or at <m>b</m>. Hence it is attained at some point
        <m>c \in (a, b)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>If <m>f(x_0) \lt f(a)</m>:</em> the minimum value of
        <m>f</m> is at most <m>f(x_0)</m>, which is less than
        <m>f(a) = f(b)</m>, so the minimum is not attained at <m>a</m>
        or at <m>b</m>. Hence it is attained at some point
        <m>c \in (a, b)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In either case, <m>f</m> has an interior extremum (a maximum or
        a minimum) at some <m>c \in (a, b)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 3: Conclude <m>f'(c) = 0</m> at the interior extremum.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose first that <m>c</m> is an interior maximum. Choose
        <m>\delta \gt 0</m> with <m>(c - \delta, c + \delta) \subset (a, b)</m>.
        For <m>0 \lt h \lt \delta</m>,
        <m>f(c + h) \leq f(c)</m>, so
        <m>(f(c+h) - f(c))/h \leq 0</m>; taking the right-hand limit,
        <m>f'(c) \leq 0</m>. For <m>-\delta \lt h \lt 0</m>,
        <m>f(c + h) \leq f(c)</m>, so
        <m>(f(c+h) - f(c))/h \geq 0</m>; taking the left-hand limit,
        <m>f'(c) \geq 0</m>. Since both inequalities hold and the
        derivative exists, <m>f'(c) = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Why may we pass to the limit in these inequalities? Taking
        these limits preserves them. For instance,
        the numbers <m>h_n = \delta/(n+1)</m> are positive, less than
        <m>\delta</m>, and tend to <m>0</m>, so by the sequential
        characterization of limits (<xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-31-3"/>) the
        quotients <m>(f(c+h_n) - f(c))/h_n</m> converge to
        <m>f'(c)</m>. Each of them is <m>\leq 0</m>, and limits of
        sequences preserve non-strict inequalities
        (<xref ref="thm-mod4-limit-order"/>), so <m>f'(c) \leq 0</m>. The
        numbers <m>-h_n</m> give <m>f'(c) \geq 0</m> in the same way.
      </p>
      <p>
        If instead <m>c</m> is an interior minimum, then <m>c</m> is an
        interior maximum of <m>-f</m>, which is differentiable on
        <m>(a, b)</m> with <m>(-f)'(c) = -f'(c)</m>. The argument above,
        applied to <m>-f</m>, gives <m>-f'(c) = 0</m>, so
        <m>f'(c) = 0</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        The proof has two cases; the key one requires <m>f</m> to be
        non-constant. The Extreme Value Theorem (from Module 6) is the
        tool that produces the interior extremum, and differentiability
        is what forces <m>f'(c) = 0</m> at that point. The argument
        for <m>f'(c) = 0</m> at an interior extremum (comparing
        left-hand and right-hand difference quotients) is itself a
        standard template in real analysis.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 4: AN MVT INEQUALITY
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod7-we-4">
    <title>An MVT Inequality</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Mean Value Theorem to prove that
        <m>|\sin(x) - \sin(y)| \leq |x - y|</m> for all
        <m>x, y \in \R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Apply the MVT to <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m> on the interval
        between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, then use the bound
        <m>|\cos(c)| \leq 1</m> to control the result. This converts
        a question about how much <m>\sin</m> can change into a
        question about how large its derivative can be.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Fix <m>x, y \in \R</m>. If <m>x = y</m>, both sides are
        <m>0</m> and the inequality holds trivially. Assume WLOG that
        <m>x \lt y</m>. The function <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m> is
        continuous on <m>[x, y]</m> and differentiable on
        <m>(x, y)</m>, with <m>f'(t) = \cos(t)</m>. By the Mean
        Value Theorem, there exists <m>c \in (x, y)</m> such that
        <md>f(y) - f(x) = f'(c)(y - x), \quad \text{i.e.,} \quad
        \sin(y) - \sin(x) = \cos(c)(y - x).</md>
        Taking absolute values:
        <md>|\sin(y) - \sin(x)| = |\cos(c)| \cdot |y - x| \leq |y - x|,</md>
        since <m>|\cos(c)| \leq 1</m> for all <m>c \in \R</m>. This
        gives <m>|\sin(x) - \sin(y)| \leq |x - y|</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        The MVT converts <q>how much does <m>\sin</m> change on
        <m>[x, y]</m>?</q> into <q>how large is <m>|\sin'(c)|</m>?</q>
        &#x2014;and the answer is at most 1. This is the prototype
        of the <em>MVT inequality template</em>:
        <m>|f(y) - f(x)| = |f'(c)| \cdot |y - x| \leq M|y-x|</m>
        whenever <m>|f'| \leq M</m> on the interval between <m>x</m>
        and <m>y</m>. In particular, a function whose derivative is
        bounded by <m>M</m> on an interval satisfies the Lipschitz
        condition <m>|f(x) - f(y)| \leq M|x - y|</m> there. By the
        argument of <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-4"/>,
        such a function is uniformly continuous. The same idea of bounding a derivative
        gives error bounds for Taylor polynomials. The MVT equality
        <m>f(y) - f(x) = f'(c)(y - x)</m> itself, without the
        inequality, is what the proof of the Fundamental Theorem of
        Calculus in MAT 5610 uses.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
